draw((0,0)--(1,0));
path t union path / H2(h1, h2, A,B);
return draw(A--B);
}
[/asy]

Call the two random numbers $x$ and $y$. Without loss of generality, we may assume that $x\leq y$.
If $x$ or $y$ is less than the $30$ degree side of an obtuse triangle, then we have $x\cdot y<1/2$. The region satisfies $x=y=x/2,$ in the $[0,1]\times[0,1]$ square. Thus, $x+y<\sqrt{\frac{1}{2}}\approx 0.7071$. (This limits the area significantly.)

The radius of the unit circle, or noting that two sides of this isosceles triangle will form is $y=\cos^{-1}(x/(2y))$. The greyed out area represents the triangles which, by the Pythagorean Theorem, have at least one $90$ degree angle and therefore are not obtuse. Using integration, we get that the shaded area (which represent the triangles with an obtuse angle) is $1-\frac{h\cdot r}{2}$. (The angle sum of a triangle is $\pi$ radians, which equals $180$ degrees.)
Thus, \begin{align*}
P=1-\frac{\int_{0}^{1/2}\cos^{-1}\left(\frac{r}{2\cos^{-1}(r)}\right)}{\int_{0}^{1} \cos^{-1}(r)}\\
